>>
endobj
404 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 199 0 R
/E (Go would win where raw performance and concurrency are critical. Here, the workload is I/O-bound \(waiting for responses from external APIs\), an area where Node's event loop is more than sufficient; the advantage of goroutines is real but yields no noticeable gain at this scale. On the downside: a second language to master, a more verbose syntax, and its absence from the list of recommended technologies.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
405 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 199 0 R
/T (Over Python)
/E (Over Python)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
406 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 199 0 R
/E (Its data/analytics ecosystem \(pandas, numpy\) is its main strength, yet this project performs no statistical computation: it aggregates and displays data from third-party APIs. That advantage is useless here, while the costs \(an additional language, lower performance\) remain.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
407 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 199 0 R
/T (In favor of Node)
/E (In favor of Node)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
408 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 199 0 R
/E (A single language across the entire stack allows TypeScript types to be shared between front end and back end \(a Widget interface defined once\), which inherently reduces integration errors. Native JSON handling makes generating about.json \(C2\) trivial.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
