478 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 201 0 R
/E (The project has two distinct needs: caching responses from external APIs and scheduling Timer refreshes \(C9\). Redis covers both with a single container, whereas Memcached or RabbitMQ would require adding a second one. Fewer services means a simpler docker-compose.yml \(C1\).)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [27]
>>
endobj
479 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 201 0 R
/T (Caching is a necessity)
/E (Caching is a necessity)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [28]
>>
endobj
480 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 201 0 R
/E (The targeted APIs \(GitHub, Steam, weather\) enforce quotas. Without caching, ten users displaying the same widget would trigger ten identical calls and hit the rate limit.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [29]
>>
endobj
481 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 201 0 R
/T (Why BullMQ)
/E (Why BullMQ)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [30]
>>
endobj
482 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 201 0 R
/E (Celery requires a Python runtime, which is inconsistent with a Node backend. node-cron handles neither job persistence \(everything is lost when the container restarts\) nor error recovery. BullMQ builds on the Redis instance already in place and supports repeatable jobs per instance.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [31]
>>
endobj
